A||||3||Roy||Pulling the engine ||||1||
Z||000000||Roy||01-24-2000||12:38 PM||||Ive decided, Im going to save my 84, by repairing her transaxle and giving the engine a rebuild. Then im going to sell it, instead of parting her out. I started pulling all the wiring and stuff off the engine. Im going to pull the engine from the top. I have the intake off, wiring, the hoses are off, the altenator and starter...ect. Im just wondering if theres anything I should watch out for when pulling the engine out of the top.<P>Roy||||reg||1
Z||000001||DRH||01-24-2000||01:58 PM||||Are you pulling the transaxle with it?<P>If so, it seems like you have most everything covered except the axles and the decklid. I pulled off the vacuum canister on the trunk wall. Also removed the trans bracket on the trunk side for more clearance as I was lifting it out. It helps to have 2 people to watch careully as you lift it out.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Roy||01-24-2000||04:46 PM||||I didn't think you could fit both the transaxle and the engine out the top? I have the decklid off. I was planning to just pull the engine and leave the transaxle in. I just need to replace the clutch and a bearing and seal on the passenger side of the transaxle.<P>Roy||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||01-24-2000||05:22 PM||||They will come out together (at least on an 84, 4 cyl, 4spd) because I did it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I did not have the intake off althought that would probably make it easier. I believe I did have the alt. mounting brackets and maybe the oil filter off. You have to twist back and forth a little to clear the deck hinge with the waterpump, but it comes pretty much straight up and out.<P>Actually, it looked to me like it would be hard to get enough seperation to get the engine out alone. Maybe on an auto, but on a manual you have to get a couple more inches for the front shaft to clear the pressure plate. Not saying it can't be done... just looked easier to pull the whole thing. Especially if you are replacing axle seals anyhow.<P>If you do go that way, pull the clutch slave cylinder off and tie it out of the way. If you don't mees with the line you won't have to bleed. Make sure nobody pushes the clutch pedal while the slave is loose though.<P>BTW... I know someone will ask why not drop the cradle... Pulling out the top seemed to suit the equipment I had on hand and stayed more in my "comfort zone". <P>David.||||reg||1
